From dff6b3e445dad57c69381960659e0d7f393d9403 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 15:18:29 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] rocket RK3576: write one constant at groups*64 instead of a
 per-weight float surface

Measured on hardware per output channel, keeping 4 bytes of that surface and
zeroing the rest leaves every channel correct, and keeping 0 leaves none. So
only the first word ever did anything. Varying it showed it is not read as a
float: flipping the sign changes nothing, doubling changes nothing, and clearing
its low byte destroys every channel. Twenty four words later the requirement is
(w & 0x3f) == 0x04 and ((w >> 6) & 0xff) >= T for some T in (0x21, 0x3f].

0x1004 satisfies it, and with it conv2d-cal, cal_s1, cal_oc16 and cal_k3 are all
correct on every output channel.

That last one is the point. The 3x3 kernel has never worked on this driver. The
old code filled the word with a dequantised weight, so whether a model computed
came down to whether its first weight happened to carry the right bits.
conv2d-cal's did and cal_k3's did not, and the kernel size was never the
dividing line at all.

The buffer size is left alone here so that content is the only change.
ROCKET_FS_FLOATS restores the old surface and ROCKET_FS_F0 overrides the word.
---
 src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c | 76 +++++++++-----------------
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+), 49 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c b/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c
index 9a4a42b..50a93a3 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c
@@ -686,62 +686,40 @@ rkt_fill_biases(struct rkt_ml_subgraph *subgraph,
                     !getenv("ROCKET_FS_TABLE_OVER") &&
                     !getenv("ROCKET_FS_HOLE")) {
             /*
-             * One fp16 per OUTPUT CHANNEL, the per-channel weight scale.
+             * ONE CONSTANT WORD at groups*64, and nothing else in the region.
              *
-             * Not a per-weight float surface. Compiling vendor .rknn files on
-             * the host and pulling this region straight out of the container
-             * says so directly:
+             * What used to be here was a float32 array with one entry per
+             * weight, MAX2(ic*oc*k*k, 8192) of them, 197888 bytes for
+             * conv2d-cal. Measured on hardware, per output channel:
              *
-             *  - compiling ONE identical model twice gives a byte identical
-             *    A/B/C table and a byte identical region here, so both are
-             *    reproducible and can be compared across models;
-             *  - dropping a model from 128 to 64 output channels leaves the
-             *    first 64 entries byte identical, so entry i belongs to
-             *    channel i;
-             *  - multiplying channel 7's weights by 2 and channel 11's by 1/2
-             *    moves exactly those two entries, by exactly 2.0 and 0.5, and
-             *    leaves the other 126 alone.
+             *   keep 1936, 1600, 1024, 512, 256, 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4 bytes
+             *       of it and zero the rest        every channel still correct
+             *   keep 0                             no channel correct
              *
-             * Read as fp16 the entries are the channel's weight scale. That
-             * also explains why zeroing this region breaks conv2d-cal while
-             * the float surface is otherwise unnecessary: the float32 weights
-             * that used to land here were being read as fp16 pairs and
-             * happened to be nonzero, and zero here scales every channel to
-             * nothing.
+             * so only the first four bytes were ever doing anything. Varying
+             * that word then showed it is not read as a float at all: flipping
+             * its sign changes nothing, doubling it changes nothing, and
+             * clearing its low byte, worth 0.0005 as a float, destroys every
+             * output channel. Twenty four words later the requirement is
              *
-             * ⚠ ON THE BOARD (round 28) THIS TABLE ALONE DOES NOT COMPUTE.
-             * conv2d-cal is 2/2 with the old float surface and 0/2 with this,
-             * and so is the relative variant. The zeroed-table control did
-             * fail, so the region IS read; the offline reading of WHICH bytes
-             * mean what is not in question. What round 28 could not tell is
-             * WHY, because it moved two things at once: it put the table at
-             * 0x400 AND left everything after it zero, and round 27 had zeroed
-             * both as well.
+             *   (w & 0x3f) == 0x04   and   ((w >> 6) & 0xff) >= T
              *
-             * ROCKET_FS_TABLE_OVER splits them. It writes the float surface
-             * exactly as the working configuration does and then overwrites
-             * only the table's own bytes, so the table is the only difference.
-             * Its complement is ROCKET_FS_HOLE, the float surface with just
-             * those bytes zeroed. Together they say whether 0x400..0x400+2*oc
-             * needs mesa's floats or whether the breakage lives after it.
+             * with T somewhere in (0x21, 0x3f]; bits 14, 15 and everything
+             * above them are free. 0x1004 satisfies it and is used here.
              *
-             * ROCKET_FS_FLOATS restores the old per-weight float surface,
-             * ROCKET_FS_REL writes the scale relative to the largest channel
-             * the way the A/B/C table's C term is written.
+             * ⚠ This is why the 3x3 kernel never worked. The old code filled
+             * that word with a dequantised weight, so whether a model computed
+             * came down to whether its FIRST WEIGHT happened to carry the right
+             * bits. conv2d-cal's did and cal_k3's did not, and every attempt to
+             * explain the difference in terms of kernel size was chasing that
+             * coincidence. With the constant, conv2d-cal, cal_s1, cal_oc16 and
+             * cal_k3 are all correct on every output channel.
+             *
+             * ROCKET_FS_FLOATS restores the old surface, ROCKET_FS_F0 sets this
+             * word to something else.
              */
-            const float *wsc = poperation->conv.weight_tensor->scales;
-            float wt_sc = poperation->conv.weight_tensor->scale;
-            float norm = 1.0f;
-            if (getenv("ROCKET_FS_REL")) {
-               norm = wsc ? wsc[0] : wt_sc;
-               if (wsc)
-                  for (unsigned oc = 1; oc < output_channels; oc++)
-                     if (wsc[oc] > norm)
-                        norm = wsc[oc];
-            }
-            uint16_t *fs16 = (uint16_t *)(b8 + groups * 64);
-            for (unsigned oc = 0; oc < output_channels; oc++)
-               fs16[oc] = _mesa_float_to_half((wsc ? wsc[oc] : wt_sc) / norm);
+            uint32_t *fs32 = (uint32_t *)(b8 + groups * 64);
+            fs32[0] = 0x1004;
          } else if (getenv("ROCKET_FS_FLOATS") ||
                     getenv("ROCKET_FS_TABLE_OVER") || getenv("ROCKET_FS_HOLE")) {
             const float *wsc = poperation->conv.weight_tensor->scales; /* per-axis */
-- 
